WEBVTT


00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.454
Anna Bogle and the Gap in the Gold. An Irish story about rain, one bright coin, and a memory that was never for sale.

00:00:10.454 --> 00:00:18.004
Ryan's tooth wobbled through breakfast, spelling, and one serious argument with an apple.

00:00:18.004 --> 00:00:22.962
By supper, it hung on through pure stubbornness.

00:00:22.962 --> 00:00:32.768
Then Ryan bit his toast and the tooth gave up. It landed in his palm, suddenly much more important than breakfast.

00:00:32.768 --> 00:00:39.650
Ryan grinned through the new gap. "How much gold is this worth?"

00:00:39.650 --> 00:00:46.939
Grandad nearly choked on his tea. "Who told you gold was the point?"

00:00:46.939 --> 00:00:58.405
Grandad leaned back. "When I was small, I heard an older, stranger version. There was a keeper named Anna Bogle."

00:00:58.405 --> 00:01:04.563
"She came for a child's tooth and left gold, but the gold was for..."

00:01:04.563 --> 00:01:10.937
Grandad stopped. The middle of the story had gone missing.

00:01:10.937 --> 00:01:16.039
Ryan folded his tooth in paper beside his pillow.

00:01:16.039 --> 00:01:22.893
Under it went a drawing of himself, Grandad, and a coin as big as a biscuit.

00:01:22.893 --> 00:01:29.435
Under a field wall, Anna Bogle's gold pouch gave a sour little clink.

00:01:29.435 --> 00:01:37.636
Anna narrowed her eyes. "Oh no. Not that shine."

00:01:37.636 --> 00:01:41.154
The curtain sneezed rain.

00:01:41.154 --> 00:01:52.044
Anna Bogle burst through with an umbrella twice her size, landed in a sock drawer, and said, "I meant to do that."

00:01:52.044 --> 00:02:08.729
Ryan sat up. "Are you Anna Bogle?" "Depends who asks." "Ryan." "Then yes. For anyone else, I am a hat."

00:02:08.729 --> 00:02:13.639
Anna lifted the tooth and softened for one breath.

00:02:13.639 --> 00:02:20.325
Then she saw the biscuit-sized coin in Ryan's drawing and snapped her mouth shut.

00:02:20.325 --> 00:02:29.459
Anna's pouch gave another cold clink. Behind it, Old Forgetting uncurled like a thread of wet smoke.

00:02:29.459 --> 00:02:38.576
A soft light touched the sill. "Anna, I came to ask," said Tanda.

00:02:38.576 --> 00:02:49.345
Anna hugged her pouch. "Ask, then. Do not turn this tooth into something to count."

00:02:49.345 --> 00:02:55.867
Tanda lowered her hands. "Then I should listen first."

00:02:55.867 --> 00:03:02.241
Anna had prepared several insults and now had nowhere tidy to put them.

00:03:02.241 --> 00:03:08.591
Grandad woke in the chair and stared at Anna, the tooth, then Tanda.

00:03:08.591 --> 00:03:16.076
"Well," he said, "my mother was not making you up."

00:03:16.076 --> 00:03:23.006
Grandad tried again. "The gold was not for buying the tooth.

00:03:23.006 --> 00:03:32.408
“It was for...” The missing part thinned like paper in rain, and Old Forgetting reached for the empty place.

00:03:32.408 --> 00:03:47.569
The shadow lifted its head. Anna planted both boots, snapped the gold shut, and said, “Back off, Old Forgetting. You do not get this one.”

00:03:47.569 --> 00:04:03.770
Ryan whispered, “What does he want?” Anna kept her eyes on the shadow. “The little details. Once those go, gold is all people remember.”

00:04:03.770 --> 00:04:17.159
Anna turned to Tanda. "You say your Tooth Fee is not a price. Show me the difference." A memory opened between them.

00:04:17.159 --> 00:04:24.785
Young Tanda covered her tooth. "If I take the coin, is it still mine?"

00:04:24.785 --> 00:04:33.011
Her father knelt. "Always. A fee is not a purchase. It is a promise to notice."

00:04:33.011 --> 00:04:45.827
The cottage returned. "My father did not buy my tooth," Tanda said. "He noticed my growing and carried it with care."

00:04:45.827 --> 00:04:56.556
Ryan frowned. "So gold is bad?" Anna looked horrified. "Gold is grand.

00:04:56.556 --> 00:05:05.114
It must stand beside the story, never on top. What should it prove?"

00:05:05.114 --> 00:05:11.732
Grandad's eyes brightened. "That someone noticed you were growing."

00:05:11.732 --> 00:05:16.762
The missing part of the old story clicked into place.

00:05:16.762 --> 00:05:24.432
The coin in Anna's pouch warmed. Not brighter than the tooth or bigger than the story.

00:05:24.432 --> 00:05:29.390
Just warm enough to say: yes, that.

00:05:29.390 --> 00:05:39.488
Ryan turned over his drawing and wrote carefully, "Grandad remembered Anna Bogle when my tooth fell out."

00:05:39.488 --> 00:05:52.496
Anna wrapped the tooth in clover cloth. "This one is not for sale," she said. "This one is for keeping."

00:05:52.496 --> 00:06:03.482
She placed a small gold piece beside the drawing, never on top. "There. Proof that somebody noticed."

00:06:03.482 --> 00:06:11.608
Tooth, note, drawing, and Ryan's exact Grandad detail gathered into a warm Toothlight.

00:06:11.608 --> 00:06:21.918
The close gold enclosure curled around the whole page. Old Forgetting shrank from every remembered detail and slipped away unfed.

00:06:21.918 --> 00:06:30.699
Tanda bowed. "Then help me ask better." Anna's gap-toothed smile appeared.

00:06:30.699 --> 00:06:38.105
"A terrible name, Network. We will discuss it."

00:06:38.105 --> 00:06:47.551
The rain thinned. Fine gold paths began at Ryan's Toothlight and reached toward other keepers and promises.

00:06:47.551 --> 00:06:57.261
Anna kept her pouch. Tanda did not ask for it. They stood watch beside Ryan's Toothlight.

00:06:57.261 --> 00:07:06.827
Morning found Ryan with a gold piece, a glowing drawing, and Grandad's whole story. The gold was lovely.

00:07:06.827 --> 00:07:16.367
Remembering was better. What did a grown-up notice when your tooth fell out? Write and draw it together.
